  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 244 PM CDT Fri Aug 28 2026\n\nAn upper ridge extends from New Mexico to Nebraska. Winds across \nsouth central and central Nebraska and north central Kansas are \nexpected to increase out of the south at around 15 to 20 MPH with \ngusts up to 25 MPH. High temperatures today will be in the mid 80s \nto mid 90s. Showers and thunderstorms are expected to approach the \narea from the west this evening and continue moving eastward \ntonight. Widespread severe storms are not expected; however, an \nisolated strong to marginally severe storm will be possible (15% \nchance). The main threat of severe storms will be along and west of \nHighway 183. Wind gusts up to around 60 MPH and hail will be the \nmain threats with these storms. Low temperatures tonight will be in \nthe 70s with winds not weakening much, if any, from the daytime. \nSkies tonight will generally be partly to mostly cloudy which will \nalso aid in preventing further cooling of temperatures overnight.\n\nThe upper ridge will move further east on Saturday with the northern \nedge extending to Nebraska. This will allow a surface trough to the \nwest (across western Nebraska and eastern Colorado) to expand \nfurther east. These conditions will allow for temperatures to heat \nup into the mid 90s to near 105 degrees on Saturday. Heat index \nvalues will generally range from the upper 90s to 105 degrees. \nIsolated areas in north central Kansas and perhaps in far southern \nNebraska will be the most likely to have temperatures and/or heat \nindex values near or at 105 degrees. Showers and perhaps an isolated \nthunderstorm or two may move across mainly western portions of the \nforecast area Saturday evening into Saturday night. Low temperatures \nSaturday night are generally expected to range from the upper 60s \nacross northwestern portions of the area to around 80 degrees across \nsouthern and southeastern portions of the area. \n\nSome showers may linger through Sunday morning and perhaps into the \nafternoon hours across portions of the area. High temperatures on \nSunday are expected to be similar to those on Saturday. However, \nportions of the area across the northwest and north on Sunday may be \na couple of degrees cooler than the previous day with increased \ncloud cover and possible developing showers due to a shortwave \npassing over the area. Heat index values on Sunday may approach or \nreach 105 degrees for isolated locations across north central \nKansas, especially Rooks, Osborne, and Mitchell Counties. A passing \nshortwave and an approaching cold front may bring showers and storms \nto mainly western and northern portions of the forecast area Sunday \nevening into Sunday night. There is a low chance (20%) that some of \nthese storms may become strong to marginally severe if there is \nenough elevated CAPE and sufficient lift. Low temperatures Sunday \nnight will range from the mid 60s to mid/upper 70s. \n\nThe upper ridge will continue to shift eastward during the early to \nmiddle part of next week. This eastward shift of the ridge will \ncontribute to temperatures cooling slightly (mid 80s to 100 degrees) \nMonday through Thursday. It will also contribute to various chances \nof showers and thunderstorms across portions of the area Monday \nthrough Thursday.",
